+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes another service for larder. This page states the trade-off to start from: favor the services
+volunteers already operate. In [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), the same argument came up for the third time that
+year, after earlier decisions about sessions and background jobs.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Add a service we operate only for a need Postgres cannot meet, with two volunteers able to restore it.
+
+This rules out adding a service just because it is a familiar solution, or because one volunteer can set it up.
+A proposal for a service volunteers would operate has to show both what Postgres cannot do and which two volunteers
+can restore the service. Start from [larder's existing architecture](architecture.md).
+
+The tenet is already applied in [keeping sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[running background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
+
+In issue 70, pantry search took four seconds and missed "Rice, brown" when searching for "rice". Before adding
+Elasticsearch, the proposer tried Postgres full-text search on the item name: it took 40 ms and found the item, so
+they chose that approach. A service's setup effort alone does not settle the cost of operating and restoring it.
+
+External services remain an option: larder uses a card gateway, and a hosted service that no volunteer has to run is
+acceptable. The tenet limits what volunteers take on operating; it does not prohibit every outside dependency.
